/* data/learn-c/mth-12-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/mth-12-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"A vector has both magnitude and direction; a scalar has magnitude only. Vectors are drawn as directed line segments and written in components along the unit vectors i, j, k. Types include the zero vector, unit vectors, equal, negative, collinear and coplanar vectors, and position vectors, which locate a point from the origin. The vector from A to B is b − a.","f":[{"x":"l = a₁/|a| , m = a₂/|a| , n = a₃/|a|","n":"Direction cosines of a vector"},{"x":"â = a/|a|","n":"Unit vector"}],"w":["AB = b − a (terminal minus initial), not a − b."],"x":["A vector is a quantity that has both magnitude (size) and direction, such as displacement, velocity, force and acceleration. A scalar has only magnitude, such as mass, time and temperature. A vector is drawn as an arrow, and written in bold (a) or with an arrow on top.","In three dimensions, a vector is written in components: a = a₁i + a₂j + a₃k, where i, j and k are the unit vectors that point along the three coordinate axes. Its magnitude is |a| = √(a₁² + a₂² + a₃²). A unit vector along a is â = a/|a|. The position vector of a point P(x, y, z) is OP = xi + yj + zk, and the vector from A to B is AB = (position vector of B) − (position vector of A).","Types of vectors: a zero vector has zero magnitude and no fixed direction. Equal vectors have the same magnitude and direction. Negative of a is −a (same size, opposite direction). Collinear (parallel) vectors lie along the same line or parallel lines: a = λb. Coplanar vectors lie in one plane. Unit vectors have magnitude 1.","Addition follows the triangle law (put the tail of the second at the head of the first) or the parallelogram law, and in components you just add the components. For multiplication by a scalar λ, the magnitude is multiplied by |λ|. The direction cosines of a are l = a₁/|a|, m = a₂/|a|, n = a₃/|a|, and l² + m² + n² = 1."],"e":"For a = 3i − 4j + 12k, |a| = √(9 + 16 + 144) = 13, the unit vector is (3i − 4j + 12k)/13, and the direction cosines are 3/13, −4/13 and 12/13."},"5.2":{"s":"The section formula gives the position vector of the point that divides the join of A(a) and B(b) in a given ratio, internally or externally. Special cases are the midpoint, (a + b)/2, and the centroid of a triangle, (a + b + c)/3, which divides each median in the ratio 2 : 1. The centroid of a tetrahedron is the average of its four vertices.","f":[{"x":"r = (k*b + a)/(k + 1)","n":"Point dividing AB internally in the ratio k : 1; externally r = (k b - a)/(k - 1).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.153"},{"x":"r = (m*b - n*a)/(m - n)","n":"External division in the ratio m : n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.153"},{"x":"g = (a + b + c)/3","n":"Centroid of triangle ABC; for a tetrahedron (a + b + c + d)/4.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.153"}],"w":["In the ratio m : n from A, m multiplies b (the far end), not a."],"x":["The section formula finds the position vector of a point that divides a line segment in a given ratio. Let A and B have position vectors a and b. A point P that divides AB internally in the ratio m : n (so that AP : PB = m : n) has the position vector r = (mb + na)/(m + n).","To remember which goes where: the point is closer to the end with the larger share of the ratio. If m is larger, P is nearer to B, so b has the larger weight, mb. Check: when m = n, r = (a + b)/2, the mid-point.","For an external division in the ratio m : n, the point lies on the line outside the segment: r = (mb − na)/(m − n). This is the same formula with n replaced by −n.","Applications: the centroid of a triangle with vertices a, b, c is (a + b + c)/3, and it divides each median in the ratio 2 : 1. The point where the diagonals of a parallelogram meet is the mid-point of both. Three points A, B, C are collinear if AB = λBC, or if C divides AB in some ratio."],"e":"A(1, 2, 3) and B(7, 8, 9): the point dividing AB in the ratio 1 : 2 is (2A + B)/3 = ((2 + 7)/3, (4 + 8)/3, (6 + 9)/3) = (3, 4, 5)."},"5.3":{"s":"The dot (scalar) product of two vectors is |a||b| cos θ, a number. It is zero for perpendicular vectors, gives the angle between two vectors, and in components is a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃. It also gives projections: the scalar projection of b on a is a·b/|a|. The dot product is commutative and distributes over addition.","f":[{"x":"a.b = |a|*|b|*cos(theta)","n":"Scalar product; 0 <= theta <= pi.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.161"},{"x":"a.b = a_1*b_1 + a_2*b_2 + a_3*b_3","n":"In components.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.162"},{"x":"a.b/|a|","n":"Scalar projection of b on a; the vector projection is (a.b/|a|^2) a.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.162"}],"w":["a·b > 0 means the angle is acute, < 0 obtuse, = 0 right angle (for non-zero vectors)."],"x":["The dot product (scalar product) of two vectors a and b is the number a · b = |a||b| cosθ, where θ is the angle between them (0 ≤ θ ≤ 180°). In components, a · b = a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃. The result is a scalar.","Properties: a · b = b · a (commutative). a · (b + c) = a · b + a · c (distributive). a · a = |a|². i · i = j · j = k · k = 1 and i · j = j · k = k · i = 0. If a · b = 0 for non-zero vectors, they are perpendicular. Also |a + b|² = |a|² + |b|² + 2a · b.","The angle between two vectors: cosθ = (a · b)/(|a||b|). The scalar projection of a on b is (a · b)/|b|, and the vector projection is ((a · b)/|b|²) b.","In physics, work done by a force F through a displacement d is W = F · d. Another use: to test for a right angle. Three points form a right-angled triangle if a dot product of two sides is zero."],"e":"a = 2i + 3j + 6k and b = i + 2j + 2k: a · b = 2 + 6 + 12 = 20, |a| = 7, |b| = 3. cosθ = 20/21, so θ = 17.75°, and the projection of a on b is 20/3."},"5.4":{"s":"The cross (vector) product a × b is a vector perpendicular to both a and b, of magnitude |a||b| sin θ, with direction by the right hand rule. It is found as a 3 × 3 determinant with i, j, k in the top row. Its magnitude is the area of the parallelogram on a and b. It is not commutative: b × a = −(a × b), and a × a = 0.","f":[{"x":"|a x b| = |a|*|b|*sin(theta)","n":"Magnitude of the vector product.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.171"},{"x":"A = |a x b|","n":"Area of the parallelogram with sides a and b; the triangle on them has half this area.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.171"}],"w":["i × j = k, j × k = i, k × i = j; reversing the order flips the sign."],"x":["The cross product (vector product) of a and b is a vector a × b with magnitude |a||b| sinθ. Its direction is perpendicular to both a and b, given by the right-hand rule: curl the fingers of the right hand from a to b, and the thumb shows a × b.","In components, a × b is the determinant of the matrix with rows (i, j, k), (a₁, a₂, a₃), (b₁, b₂, b₃): a × b = (a₂b₃ − a₃b₂)i − (a₁b₃ − a₃b₁)j + (a₁b₂ − a₂b₁)k.","Properties: a × b = −(b × a) (anti-commutative, so order matters). a × a = 0. a × b = 0 if the two are parallel. a × (b + c) = a × b + a × c. i × j = k, j × k = i, k × i = j. Also |a × b|² + (a · b)² = |a|²|b|² (Lagrange's identity).","Geometry: |a × b| is the area of the parallelogram with adjacent sides a and b, and ½|a × b| is the area of the triangle. A unit vector perpendicular to both a and b is ±(a × b)/|a × b|. In physics, torque is r × F, angular momentum is r × p, and the force on a moving charge is qv × B."],"e":"a = i + 2j + 3k, b = 3i − j + 2k: a × b = (4 + 3)i − (2 − 9)j + (−1 − 6)k = 7i + 7j − 7k, with |a × b| = 7√3 = 12.12. The triangle area would be 6.06."},"5.5":{"s":"The scalar triple product [a b c] = a · (b × c) equals the 3 × 3 determinant of the components. It is the volume of the parallelepiped with coterminus edges a, b, c, and one sixth of it is the volume of the tetrahedron. It is zero when the three vectors are coplanar (or any two are collinear). Swapping two vectors changes its sign; swapping dot and cross does not change it.","f":[{"x":"V = a.(b x c) = [a b c]","n":"Volume of the parallelepiped with coterminus edges a, b, c (take the absolute value).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.180"},{"x":"V = (1/6)*[a b c]","n":"Volume of the tetrahedron with coterminus edges a, b, c.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.180"}],"w":["Three vectors are coplanar exactly when [a b c] = 0."],"x":["The scalar triple product of three vectors is a · (b × c), written [a b c]. In components it is the 3 × 3 determinant with the rows of components of a, b and c. It is a number.","Geometrically, |a · (b × c)| is the volume of the parallelepiped with edges a, b and c: b × c has the size of the area of the base, and the dot product with a multiplies it by the height. The volume of the tetrahedron with these three edges is one-sixth of that.","Properties: a cyclic change of order does not change the value, [a b c] = [b c a] = [c a b]. Swapping two vectors changes the sign. If two of the vectors are equal or parallel, the value is zero. The dot and the cross may be interchanged, a · (b × c) = (a × b) · c. Three vectors are coplanar if and only if [a b c] = 0. Four points are coplanar if the three vectors joining one of them to the others are coplanar.","The vector triple product is a × (b × c) = (a · c)b − (a · b)c ('BAC minus CAB'). It is a vector in the plane of b and c. Note that a × (b × c) is not equal to (a × b) × c in general."],"e":"a = i + 2j + 3k, b = j + 4k, c = 5i + 6j: [a b c] = 1·(0 − 24) − 2·(0 − 20) + 3·(0 − 5) = −24 + 40 − 15 = 1. The parallelepiped has volume 1, and the vectors are not coplanar."}};
